86045417] [17592186490233 #edb/ref 1074 "The ratio of pages to days is $45:6.$ Expressing this as a fraction, we have:\n\n$$\n\\frac{45}{6}\n$$\n\nWe can reduce this fraction to its lowest terms by dividing the numerator and denominator by $3:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{45÷3}{6÷3}=\\frac{15}{2}\n$$\n\nConsequently, Steven read the book at a rate of $\\frac{15}{2}$ pages per day." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490252 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490102 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490252 #edb/ref 1069 "q-60454" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490252 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490252 #edb/ref 1071 "Sophia bought $14$ peaches for $$4.$ What is the price per peach?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490252 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "585e1f16-1ef5-574f-9665-67875bdab00b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490252 #edb/ref 1074 "The ratio of dollars to peaches is $4:14.$ Expressing this as a fraction, we have:\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{14}\n$$\n\nWe can reduce this fraction to its lowest terms by dividing the numerator and denominator by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{4÷2}{14÷2}=\\frac{2}{7}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the price of each peach is $\\frac{2}{7}$ of one dollar." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490263 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490109 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490263 #edb/ref 1069 "q-60438" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490263 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490263 #edb/ref 1071 "Elena writes $150$ words in $3$ minutes. If she writes at a steady pace, then what statement describes the unit rate?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490263 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6f0e951c-f617-594c-9180-7fe86b2cb1e4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490263 #edb/ref 1074 "The ratio of written words to minutes is $150:3.$ So we start with the ratio $150:3$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{150}{3}\n$$\n\nWe can make a denominator of $1$ by dividing the numerator and denominator by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{150÷3}{3÷3}=\\frac{50}{1}\n$$\n\nConsequently, Elena writes $50$ words per minute." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490273 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490168 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490273 #edb/ref 1069 "q-92871" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490273 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490273 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the missing number in the statement below?\n\n$\\,$ $34$ books in $2$ shelves $=\\boxed{}\\,$  books per shelf." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490273 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "87954c9a-1409-5d07-8491-76a8bb88c183" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490273 #edb/ref 1074 "The ratio of the number of books to shelves is $34:2.$ So we start with the ratio $34:2$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{34}{2}\n$$\n\nWe can make a denominator of $1$ by dividing the numerator and denominator by $2$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{34÷2}{2÷2}=\\frac{17}{1}=17\n$$\n\nSo, $34$ books in $2$ shelves $=17$ books per shelf." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490281 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490140 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490281 #edb/ref 1069 "q-60443" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490281 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490281 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the missing number in the statement below?\n\n$\\,$ $12$ dogs in $6$ houses $=\\boxed{}\\,$  dogs per house." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490281 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9f8b2137-ad47-5a05-9270-4407d12097e6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490281 #edb/ref 1074 "The ratio of the number of dogs to the number of houses is $12:6.$ So we start with the ratio $12:6$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{12}{6}\n$$\n\nWe can make a denominator of $1$ by dividing the numerator and denominator by $6$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{12÷6}{6÷6}=\\frac{2}{1}=2\n$$\n\nSo, $12$ dogs in $6$ houses $=2$ dogs per house." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490283 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490074 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490283 #edb/ref 1069 "q-60441" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490283 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490283 #edb/ref 1071 "Daniel bought $8$ cookies for $$4.$ What statement describes the unit rate?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490283 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a2b1554d-f282-5078-95da-a93d4133cf2c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490283 #edb/ref 1074 "The ratio of cookies to dollars is $8:4.$ So we start with the ratio $8:4$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{8}{4}\n$$\n\nWe can make a denominator of $1$ by dividing the numerator and denominator by $4$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{8÷4}{4÷4}=\\frac{2}{1}\n$$\n\nConsequently, Daniel bought $2$ cookies per dollar." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490292 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490094 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490292 #edb/ref 1069 "q-169435" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490292 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490292 #edb/ref 1071 "Lily bought $8$ small cakes for $$12.$ At what unit price did Lily pay?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490292 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b262ffc7-3f09-5b66-b29a-de72ce27ab0c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490292 #edb/ref 1074 "The ratio of dollars to cakes is $12:8.$ Expressing this as a fraction, we have:\n\n$$\n\\frac{12}{8}\n$$\n\nWe can reduce this fraction to its lowest terms by dividing the numerator and denominator by $4:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{12÷4}{8÷4}=\\frac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the unit price Lily paid is $\\frac{3}{2}$ of a dollar per cake." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490293 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490130 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490293 #edb/ref 1069 "q-60053" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490293 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490293 #edb/ref 1071 "John bought a bouquet of $4$ roses for $$5.$ What is the price per rose?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490293 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b842ef2a-5852-5df6-832b-3f42a5693c4d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490293 #edb/ref 1074 "The ratio of dollars to roses is $5:4.$ Expressing this as a fraction, we have:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{4}\n$$\n\nWe can convert this fraction to a decimal by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $25:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{5\\times 25}{4\\times 25}=\\frac{125}{100}=1.25\n$$\n\nConsequently, the cost is $$1.25$ per rose." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490297 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490096 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490297 #edb/ref 1069 "q-92896" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490297 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490297 #edb/ref 1071 "John bought $25$ candies for $$3.$ What is the price per candy?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490297 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bc23fb40-881c-569e-9c33-6084a7f24a37" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490297 #edb/ref 1074 "The ratio of dollars to candies is $3:25.$ Expressing this as a fraction, we have:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{25}\n$$\n\nWe can convert this fraction to a decimal by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $4:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{3\\times 4}{25\\times 4}=\\frac{12}{100}=0.12\n$$\n\nConsequently, the cost is $$0.12$ per candy." #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
